--- file style.css
{{asset:appCss}}

--- default js
// app.js: the JavaScript for the Friends page.
// Lines that start with // are comments. JavaScript skips them.


=== step hello
title: Hello, Console
minutes: 10
slot: js
--- explain
**JavaScript** is a list of instructions for the browser. It runs them one at a time, from the top of the file to the bottom.

Your first instruction writes a message:

```js
console.log("Good morning, Springfield!");
```

- `console.log( )` means "write this in the Console".
- The message goes inside the brackets, in quotes.
- The semicolon `;` ends the instruction, like a full stop ends a sentence.

The **Console** is a message panel for developers. Visitors never see it, but you'll use it all day to look inside your code. In the Lab it's **under the preview, on the right**. In a normal browser you open it with the **F12** key.

> Every time you stop typing, the Lab runs your code again, and the Console starts fresh.
--- task
1. Under the comments in the code box, write a `console.log` that says **Hello, ShowShelf!**
2. On the next line, write a second `console.log` with a sentence about a show you like, for example "My favourite show is Dark."

Look at the Console: two lines, in the same order as your code.
--- hint
Copy the example above and change the text between the quotes. Keep the quotes and the brackets.
--- hint
```js
console.log("Hello, ShowShelf!");
console.log("My favourite show is Dark.");
```
--- solution-append
console.log("Hello, ShowShelf!");
console.log("My favourite show is Dark.");
--- check
if (t.errors.length) {
  t.expect(!/Console is not defined/.test(t.errors[0]), "JavaScript cares about capital letters: write `console` with a small c.");
  t.expect(!/is not defined/.test(t.errors[0]) || /["'`]/.test(t.clean), "Put your message in quotes: `console.log(\"Hello, ShowShelf!\");`. Without quotes, JavaScript thinks the words are names of things.");
  t.fail(`There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the grey help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
}
const lines = t.logs.filter((l) => l.level === "log" && l.text.trim());
if (!lines.length) {
  t.expect(!/console\s*\.\s*log\s*\(\s*\)/.test(t.clean), "Your `console.log()` is empty. Put a message in quotes between the brackets.");
  t.expect(!/console\s*\.\s*log\b/i.test(t.code) || /console\s*\.\s*log/.test(t.clean), "Your `console.log` is on a comment line (it starts with `//`), so JavaScript skips it. Write it on a new line without `//`.");
  t.fail("Nothing appeared in the Console yet. Write `console.log(\"Hello, ShowShelf!\");` in the code box.");
}
t.expect(lines.some((l) => /hello/i.test(l.text)), `The Console says "${lines[0].text}". Make your first message say **Hello, ShowShelf!**`);
t.expect(lines.length >= 2, "The hello message is there. Now add a second `console.log` line with a sentence about a show you like.");
--- learned
You gave the browser its first instructions. `console.log` is your window into the code: whenever you wonder what's going on, log it.
--- summary
`console.log("...")` writes a message in the Console. Code runs from top to bottom.
--- mistakes
- `Console.log` with a capital C, or `console.Log`.
- Forgetting the quotes: `console.log(Hello)`.
- Looking for the message on the page instead of in the Console.
--- teacher
If the Console panel is hidden, click its title bar under the preview. Show students that the order of the lines in the Console follows the order in the code.


=== step types
title: Text and numbers
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
Every piece of data in JavaScript is a **value**, and values have **types**. Today you meet the two most common ones:

- A **string** is text. It always goes in quotes: `"Dark"`. Double or single quotes both work: `'Dark'`.
- A **number** has no quotes: `26`, `8.7`. Decimals use a dot, not a comma.

JavaScript can do maths with numbers, so `console.log(2 + 3)` prints `5`. But the `+` sign does something different with strings: it **joins** them.

```js
console.log(201 + 1);      // 202
console.log("201" + 1);    // 2011
console.log("The" + " Office");   // The Office
```

In the second line the quotes turned `201` into text, and text plus anything makes longer text. This surprises everyone once. It's also why the difference between strings and numbers matters.

> The grey `// 202` at the end of a line is a comment: a note for you, skipped by JavaScript.
--- task
Under your two hello lines:

1. Log `236 + 62` (the episodes of Friends plus the episodes of Breaking Bad). No quotes.
2. Log `"236" + 62`, with quotes around the first number only. What do you get, and why?
--- hint
Each one is its own `console.log( )` line. In the first, the numbers have no quotes at all.
--- hint
```js
console.log(236 + 62);
console.log("236" + 62);
```
--- solution-append
console.log(236 + 62);
console.log("236" + 62);
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
const lines = t.logs.map((l) => l.text.trim());
if (!lines.includes("298")) {
  t.expect(!lines.some((x) => /236\s*\+\s*62/.test(x)), "The Console shows `236 + 62` as text, because it's inside quotes. Remove the quotes so JavaScript does the maths: `console.log(236 + 62);`.");
  t.fail("I can't see **298** in the Console. Add `console.log(236 + 62);` with no quotes.");
}
t.expect(lines.includes("23662"), "Now log `\"236\" + 62`: quotes around 236 only. Look at what the Console shows.");
--- learned
Numbers add up, strings join together. `"236" + 62` gives `23662` because the first value is text. Keep this in mind: numbers that come from web pages often arrive as text.
--- summary
Strings are text in quotes, numbers have no quotes. `+` adds numbers but joins strings.
--- mistakes
- Quotes around the whole sum: `console.log("236 + 62")` prints the text itself.
- A comma in decimals: `8,7` instead of `8.7`.


=== step const
title: Variables with const
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
A **variable** is a name for a value, like a label on a box. You create one with `const`:

```js
const drink = "coffee";
const cups = 3;

console.log(drink);   // coffee
console.log(cups);    // 3
```

Read the first line as: "create a box called `drink` and put `"coffee"` in it". The `=` means *put into*, not *is equal to*.

Rules for names:

- no spaces, and no quotes around the name
- start with a letter
- for names with several words, start each new word with a capital: `showName`, `episodesWatched`. This is called **camelCase**.
- capitals matter: `showName` and `showname` are two different names

When you use a variable, write its name **without quotes**. `console.log(cups)` prints `3`; `console.log("cups")` prints the word *cups*.
--- task
Under your other lines, create three variables about Friends:

1. `showName` with the text `"Friends"`
2. `episodes` with the number `236`
3. `runtime` with the number `30` (minutes per episode)

Then log `showName` and log `episodes`.
--- hint
Each variable is one line: `const`, the name, `=`, the value, `;`. Strings in quotes, numbers without.
--- hint
```js
const showName = "Friends";
const episodes = 236;
const runtime = 30;

console.log(showName);
console.log(episodes);
```
--- solution-append
const showName = "Friends";
const episodes = 236;
const runtime = 30;

console.log(showName);
console.log(episodes);
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
const name = t.get("showName"), ep = t.get("episodes"), rt = t.get("runtime");
if (name === undefined) {
  t.expect(!/const\s+showname\b/i.test(t.clean), "Check the capitals: the name is `showName`, with a capital N.");
  t.fail("I can't find a variable called `showName`. Create it with `const showName = \"Friends\";`.");
}
t.expect(typeof name === "string", "`showName` should be text, in quotes: `\"Friends\"`.");
t.expect(name.trim().toLowerCase() === "friends", `\`showName\` contains "${name}". It should be "Friends".`);
t.expect(ep !== undefined, "Now create `episodes`: `const episodes = 236;`.");
t.expect(typeof ep === "number", "`episodes` is text because of the quotes. Numbers have no quotes: `const episodes = 236;`.");
t.expect(ep === 236, `\`episodes\` is ${ep}. Friends has 236 episodes.`);
t.expect(rt !== undefined, "Now create `runtime`: `const runtime = 30;`.");
t.expect(typeof rt === "number", "`runtime` should be a number, without quotes: `const runtime = 30;`.");
t.expect(rt === 30, `\`runtime\` is ${rt}. Use 30 (minutes per episode).`);
const lines = t.logs.map((l) => l.text.trim());
if (!lines.some((x) => x.toLowerCase() === "friends")) {
  t.expect(!lines.includes("showName"), "The Console shows the word *showName*. Remove the quotes: `console.log(showName);` prints what's inside the variable.");
  t.fail("Log the variable: `console.log(showName);`, with no quotes around the name.");
}
t.expect(lines.includes("236"), "Now log the number of episodes: `console.log(episodes);`.");
--- learned
Variables give values a name, so you can use them again and again. From now on you'll almost always work with names like `episodes` instead of typing the numbers.
--- summary
`const name = value;` creates a variable. Use its name without quotes.
--- mistakes
- Quotes around the variable when using it: `console.log("showName")`.
- Quotes around numbers: `const episodes = "236";`.
- A space in the name: `const show name`.


=== step let
title: Values that change: let
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
A `const` can never get a new value. That's useful: you know it stays the same. But some values *should* change, like how many episodes you've watched. For those, use `let`:

```js
let cupsToday = 1;
console.log(cupsToday);   // 1

cupsToday = cupsToday + 1;
console.log(cupsToday);   // 2
```

To give a `let` variable a new value, write its name, `=` and the new value. Don't write `let` again: that would try to create a second box with the same name.

Read `cupsToday = cupsToday + 1;` from right to left: take what's in `cupsToday` (1), add 1, and put the result (2) back into `cupsToday`.

> Shorter ways to add 1, which you'll see in other people's code: `cupsToday += 1;` and `cupsToday++;`.

A good habit: use `const` unless you know the value will change.
--- task
1. Create `episodesWatched` with `let`, starting at `10`, and log it.
2. On a new line, add 1 to it. Then log it again. The Console should show 10, then 11.
3. Try this: change `let` to `const` and read the red error. Then change it back.
--- hint
Adding 1 is a line that starts with the name (no `let`): `episodesWatched = episodesWatched + 1;`.
--- hint
```js
let episodesWatched = 10;
console.log(episodesWatched);

episodesWatched = episodesWatched + 1;
console.log(episodesWatched);
```
--- solution-append
let episodesWatched = 10;
console.log(episodesWatched);

episodesWatched = episodesWatched + 1;
console.log(episodesWatched);
--- check
if (t.errors.length) {
  t.expect(!/constant/i.test(t.errors[0]), "That's the error you get when you change a `const`. Well spotted! Now change `const episodesWatched` back to `let episodesWatched`.");
  t.expect(!/already been declared/.test(t.errors[0]), "`episodesWatched` is created twice. Write `let` only the first time. To change it, write only the name: `episodesWatched = episodesWatched + 1;`.");
  t.fail(`There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
}
const w = t.get("episodesWatched");
t.expect(w !== undefined, "I can't find `episodesWatched`. Create it with `let episodesWatched = 10;` (capital W).");
t.expect(/\blet\s+episodesWatched\b/.test(t.clean), "Create `episodesWatched` with `let`, because its value will change.");
t.expect(typeof w === "number", "`episodesWatched` should be a number, without quotes.");
t.expect(/^\s*episodesWatched\s*(\+\+|\+=|=[^=])/m.test(t.clean), "Now add 1 on a new line that starts with the name: `episodesWatched = episodesWatched + 1;`.");
t.expect(w === 11, `After adding 1, \`episodesWatched\` is ${w}. Start at 10 and add 1, so it ends at 11.`);
const lines = t.logs.map((l) => l.text.trim());
t.expect(lines.includes("10") && lines.includes("11"), "Log `episodesWatched` twice: once before you add 1 and once after. The Console should show 10 and then 11.");
--- learned
`let` makes a variable you can change; `const` makes one you can't. The same name printed twice gave two different values, because the code between the two logs changed it.
--- summary
`let` for values that change. Change them with `name = new value;`.
--- mistakes
- Writing `let` again on the second line: "has already been declared".
- Writing `episodesWatched + 1;` alone (it calculates 11 but doesn't store it).
- Using `const` and getting "Assignment to constant variable".


=== step maths
title: Maths with variables
minutes: 20
slot: js
--- explain
JavaScript is a good calculator. The **operators** are `+` and `-`, `*` for times, and `/` for divided by. The order is the same as at school: `*` and `/` before `+` and `-`, and brackets first.

The best part: you can calculate with variables, and store the result in a new variable.

```js
const bbEpisodes = 62;
const bbRuntime = 60;
const bbMinutes = bbEpisodes * bbRuntime;
console.log(bbMinutes);        // 3720
console.log(bbMinutes / 60);   // 62
```

Division often gives long decimals. **Math.round** rounds to the nearest whole number:

```js
console.log(Math.round(4.6));  // 5
console.log(Math.round(4.4));  // 4
```

`Math` is a toolbox that comes with JavaScript. You'll meet more of its tools later.

> Why variables and not numbers? If you change `runtime` one day, every result that uses it updates by itself.
--- task
Under your code, using your variables `episodes` and `runtime`:

1. Create `totalMinutes`: episodes times runtime. Log it.
2. Create `hours`: totalMinutes divided by 60. Log it.
3. Create `days`: hours divided by 24. Log it. (Lots of decimals!)
4. Log `Math.round(days)`.
--- hint
Each result is a new `const`. For example `const totalMinutes = episodes * runtime;`, then `console.log(totalMinutes);`.
--- hint
```js
const totalMinutes = episodes * runtime;
console.log(totalMinutes);

const hours = totalMinutes / 60;
console.log(hours);

const days = hours / 24;
console.log(days);
console.log(Math.round(days));
```
--- solution-append
const totalMinutes = episodes * runtime;
console.log(totalMinutes);

const hours = totalMinutes / 60;
console.log(hours);

const days = hours / 24;
console.log(days);
console.log(Math.round(days));
--- check
t.expect(!/math is not defined/.test(t.errors[0] || ""), "JavaScript's toolbox is called `Math`, with a capital M: `Math.round(days)`.");
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
const tm = t.get("totalMinutes"), h = t.get("hours"), d = t.get("days");
t.expect(tm !== undefined, "I can't find `totalMinutes`. Create it with `const totalMinutes = episodes * runtime;`.");
t.expect(tm === 7080, `\`totalMinutes\` is ${JSON.stringify(tm)}. It should be episodes times runtime (236 × 30 = 7080). Use \`*\` for times.`);
t.expect(h !== undefined, "Now create `hours`: `const hours = totalMinutes / 60;`.");
t.expect(h === 118, `\`hours\` is ${JSON.stringify(h)}. It should be totalMinutes divided by 60 (118).`);
t.expect(d !== undefined, "Now create `days`: `const days = hours / 24;`.");
t.expect(typeof d === "number" && Math.abs(d - 118 / 24) < 0.01, `\`days\` is ${JSON.stringify(d)}. It should be hours divided by 24 (about 4.9).`);
const lines = t.logs.map((l) => l.text.trim());
t.expect(lines.includes("7080") && lines.includes("118"), "Log each result: `console.log(totalMinutes);` and `console.log(hours);`.");
t.expect(/Math\.round\s*\(/.test(t.clean), "Last one: log the rounded number of days with `console.log(Math.round(days));`. Capital M.");
t.expect(lines.includes("5"), "Log the result of `Math.round(days)`: `console.log(Math.round(days));`. The Console should show 5.");
--- learned
You calculated with variables and stored the results in new ones. Watching all of Friends takes 118 hours: almost five whole days without sleep.
--- summary
Maths: `+ - * /`, results in variables. `Math.round()` rounds to a whole number.
--- mistakes
- `math.round` with a small m.
- `x` instead of `*` for times.
- Typing the numbers (`236 * 30`) instead of the variable names.


=== step template
title: Sentences with backticks
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
You can build a sentence by joining strings and variables with `+`, but it gets messy fast:

```js
console.log("I'd like " + cups + " cups of " + drink + ", please.");
```

A **template literal** is easier to read. It uses **backticks** instead of quotes (look closely at the first and last character inside the brackets below), and puts each variable inside `${ }`:

```js
console.log(`I'd like ${cups} cups of ${drink}, please.`);
// I'd like 3 cups of coffee, please.
```

Everything inside `${ }` is replaced by its value. Everything else stays as you typed it, spaces included.

> **Where's the backtick key?** On most keyboards it's at the top left, under Esc, next to the 1. On some keyboards you press it and then the space bar.
--- task
Create a variable `sentence` with a template literal that makes this text from your variables:

**Watching all 236 episodes of Friends takes 118 hours.**

Use `${episodes}`, `${showName}` and `${hours}` for the parts that come from variables. Then log `sentence`.
--- hint
Start with `const sentence =`, then a backtick, the sentence, another backtick and `;`. In the sentence, replace each number and the name with `${...}`.
--- hint
```js
const sentence = `Watching all ${episodes} episodes of ${showName} takes ${hours} hours.`;
console.log(sentence);
```
--- solution-append
const sentence = `Watching all ${episodes} episodes of ${showName} takes ${hours} hours.`;
console.log(sentence);
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
const s = t.get("sentence");
t.expect(s !== undefined, "I can't find a variable called `sentence`. Create it with `const sentence =` and your text between backticks.");
t.expect(typeof s === "string", "`sentence` should be text, made with backticks.");
t.expect(!/\$\{/.test(s), `Your sentence shows "${s}" with the \`\${ }\` still in it. Use backticks around the text, not quotes: only backticks fill in \`\${ }\`. The backtick key is usually at the top left, under Esc.`);
t.expect(/friends/i.test(s), "Your sentence doesn't mention Friends. Put `${showName}` where the name goes.");
t.expect(/\b118\b/.test(s), "Your sentence doesn't show 118. Put `${hours}` where the number of hours goes.");
t.expect(/\b236\b/.test(s), "Your sentence doesn't show 236. Put `${episodes}` where the number of episodes goes.");
t.expect(/\$\{\s*\w+\s*\}/.test(t.clean), "Use `${ }` with your variables, instead of typing the numbers yourself.");
t.expect(t.logs.some((l) => l.text === s), "Log it: `console.log(sentence);`.");
--- learned
Template literals put values into text: backticks around it all, `${ }` around each variable. You'll use them every day from now on.
--- summary
Template literals: text between backticks, with `${variable}` for the values.
--- mistakes
- Normal quotes instead of backticks: the `${ }` is printed as it is.
- `$(hours)` or `{hours}` instead of `${hours}`.
- Forgetting spaces around the `${ }` parts, so words stick together.


=== step page
title: Put text on the page
minutes: 20
slot: js
--- explain
The Console is for you. Now let's show something to your visitors. Two pieces of JavaScript do it:

```js
document.querySelector("#greeting").textContent = "Welcome back!";
```

Read it from left to right:

- `document` is the web page.
- `.querySelector("#greeting")` **finds** the element with `id="greeting"`. The `#` works just like in CSS.
- `.textContent = ...` **changes its text** to the value on the right.

So: *in the page, find #greeting, and set its text to "Welcome back!"*

Open the **show.html (whole page)** tab. The grey notes in the preview are empty elements with ids: `title`, `meta`, `watch-time`. Your JavaScript can fill them.

> On Day 7 you'll learn much more about finding and changing elements. Today, this one pattern is enough.
--- task
At the bottom of your code, fill three elements:

1. `#title` gets `showName`
2. `#meta` gets a template literal: **236 episodes · 30 minutes each** (use `${episodes}` and `${runtime}`)
3. `#watch-time` gets your `sentence`

Watch the grey notes disappear.
--- hint
Each one is a line like the example: `document.querySelector("#title").textContent = showName;`. Change the id and the value on the right.
--- hint
```js
document.querySelector("#title").textContent = showName;
document.querySelector("#meta").textContent = `${episodes} episodes · ${runtime} minutes each`;
document.querySelector("#watch-time").textContent = sentence;
```
--- solution-append
document.querySelector("#title").textContent = showName;
document.querySelector("#meta").textContent = `${episodes} episodes · ${runtime} minutes each`;
document.querySelector("#watch-time").textContent = sentence;
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
t.expect(!/\.(?!textContent\b)[tT]ext[cC]ontent\b/.test(t.clean), "Check the capitals: it's `textContent`, with a capital C. With other spelling, nothing happens and there's no error.");
const title = t.text("#title");
if (!title) {
  t.expect(!/querySelector\(\s*["']title["']\s*\)/.test(t.clean), "`querySelector(\"title\")` without `#` finds the page's `<title>` tag (the text on the browser tab). Add the `#`: `querySelector(\"#title\")`.");
  t.fail("The title is still empty. Add `document.querySelector(\"#title\").textContent = showName;`.");
}
t.expect(title.toLowerCase() === "friends", `The title says "${title}". Set it to the variable \`showName\`, without quotes.`);
const meta = t.text("#meta");
t.expect(meta, "Now fill `#meta` with a template literal that uses `${episodes}` and `${runtime}`.");
t.expect(!/\$\{/.test(meta), "The facts line shows `${ }`. Use backticks around the text, not quotes.");
t.expect(/236/.test(meta) && /30/.test(meta), `The facts line says "${meta}". It should show 236 episodes and 30 minutes.`);
const wt = t.text("#watch-time");
t.expect(wt, "Last one: fill `#watch-time` with your `sentence`.");
t.expect(/118/.test(wt), `\`#watch-time\` says "${wt}". Set it to your variable \`sentence\`.`);
--- learned
Your JavaScript changed the page! `document.querySelector` finds an element, and `.textContent` replaces its text. That's the bridge between your code and what visitors see.
--- summary
`document.querySelector("#id").textContent = value;` puts text on the page.
--- mistakes
- Forgetting the `#` in the selector: `querySelector("title")` changes the browser tab's title instead.
- `textcontent` with a small c: no error, but nothing happens.
- Quotes around the variable: the page shows the word *showName*.


=== step booleans
title: True or false
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
A third type of value has only two possible values: `true` and `false`. It's called a **boolean**, and it's the answer to a yes-or-no question.

You get booleans by **comparing**:

- `>` and `<`: greater than, less than
- `>=` and `<=`: greater than or equal to, less than or equal to
- `===`: is equal to
- `!==`: is not equal to

```js
const cups = 3;
console.log(cups > 2);      // true
console.log(cups === 4);    // false
```

Careful with the equals signs: one `=` *puts a value into* a variable; three `===` *ask* whether two values are the same.

`===` is strict: the type must match too. The number `30` and the string `"30"` are **not** equal.
--- task
1. Create `isLong`: whether `episodes` is greater than 100. Log it.
2. Log `showName === "Friends"`.
3. Log `runtime === "30"`. Why is the answer `false`?
--- hint
`const isLong = episodes > 100;` stores the answer to the question "is episodes more than 100?". The other two go straight into `console.log( )`.
--- hint
```js
const isLong = episodes > 100;
console.log(isLong);
console.log(showName === "Friends");
console.log(runtime === "30");
```
--- solution-append
const isLong = episodes > 100;
console.log(isLong);
console.log(showName === "Friends");
console.log(runtime === "30");
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it, fix that line, then check again.`);
const long = t.get("isLong");
t.expect(long !== undefined, "I can't find `isLong`. Create it with `const isLong = episodes > 100;`.");
t.expect(typeof long === "boolean", `\`isLong\` is ${JSON.stringify(long)}. It should be the answer to a comparison, without quotes: \`episodes > 100\`.`);
t.expect(long === true, "`isLong` is false, but 236 is more than 100. Check the comparison: `episodes > 100`.");
const lines = t.logs.map((l) => l.text.trim());
t.expect(lines.includes("true"), "Log `isLong`: `console.log(isLong);`.");
t.expect(lines.filter((x) => x === "true").length >= 2, "Log the comparison `showName === \"Friends\"`. It should print `true`.");
t.expect(lines.includes("false"), "Log `runtime === \"30\"` too. It prints `false`: `runtime` holds the number 30, not the text \"30\".");
--- learned
Comparisons answer with `true` or `false`. And `===` checks the type too: the number 30 and the text "30" are different values.
--- summary
Booleans are `true` or `false`. Compare with `> < >= <= === !==`.
--- mistakes
- One `=` in a comparison: `showName = "Friends"` tries to change the variable.
- Quotes around `true`: `"true"` is a string, not a boolean.


=== step if-else
title: Choose with if and else
minutes: 20
slot: js
--- explain
Now your code can make decisions. `if` runs a block of code only when a condition is `true`. `else` runs the other block when it's `false`:

```js
if (cups > 2) {
  console.log("That's a lot of coffee.");
} else {
  console.log("Room for one more cup.");
}
```

- The **condition** goes in round brackets `( )`.
- Each **block** of code goes in curly brackets `{ }`.
- Exactly one of the two blocks runs, never both.

Indent the lines inside each block with two spaces (press Tab). It doesn't change what the code does, but it shows at a glance which lines belong to which block.

You already have a boolean ready to use: `isLong`. A condition can be a comparison like `episodes > 100`, or a variable that holds `true` or `false`.
--- task
At the bottom of your code, write an `if / else`:

- If the show is long (`isLong`), put **A long one: get comfortable on the sofa!** in `#verdict`.
- Else, put **A short one: perfect for a weekend.** in `#verdict`.

Then change `episodes` to `10` at the top and watch the message change. Change it back to `236` when you're done.
--- hint
Inside each pair of `{ }`, write one line that changes `#verdict`, like you did in step 7: `document.querySelector("#verdict").textContent = "...";`.
--- hint
```js
if (isLong) {
  document.querySelector("#verdict").textContent = "A long one: get comfortable on the sofa!";
} else {
  document.querySelector("#verdict").textContent = "A short one: perfect for a weekend.";
}
```
--- solution-append
if (isLong) {
  document.querySelector("#verdict").textContent = "A long one: get comfortable on the sofa!";
} else {
  document.querySelector("#verdict").textContent = "A short one: perfect for a weekend.";
}
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Look for a missing bracket \`)\` or \`}\` near the line it names.`);
t.expect(/\bif\s*\(/.test(t.clean), "Start with `if (isLong) {`: the word `if`, then the condition in round brackets.");
t.expect(/\}\s*else\s*\{/.test(t.clean), "Add the `else { ... }` part after the closing `}` of the `if` block: what should the page say when the show is short?");
const v = t.text("#verdict");
t.expect(v, "`#verdict` is still empty. Inside the `if` block, put a line that sets the text of `#verdict`.");
t.expect(t.get("episodes") === 236, "Change `episodes` back to 236 before you check.");
t.expect(!/short/i.test(v) || /long/i.test(v), `\`#verdict\` says "${v}", but Friends is a long show. The long message belongs in the \`if\` block, the short one in the \`else\` block.`);
// try the same code with a short show, to test the else block
const m = t.code.match(/\b(const|let)\s+episodes\s*=\s*[^;\n]+/);
let other = null;
if (m) {
  try {
    new t.win.Function(t.code.replace(m[0], m[1] + " episodes = 5"))();
    other = t.text("#verdict");
  } catch (e) { other = null; }
  await t.rerun();
}
t.expect(other === null || other !== v, `With a short show (5 episodes), \`#verdict\` still says "${other}". Check that your \`if\` uses \`isLong\` (or \`episodes > 100\`) and that the \`else\` block sets a different message.`);
--- learned
Your page now makes a decision: the same code shows a different message when the numbers change. That's what makes programs more than a list of fixed sentences.
--- summary
`if (condition) { ... } else { ... }` runs one of two blocks.
--- mistakes
- A semicolon after the condition: `if (isLong); {` (the block then always runs).
- Missing `}` before `else`.
- Putting both messages outside the blocks, so the second one always wins.
--- teacher
The check runs the student's code once more with `episodes` set to 5 to test the `else` block, so a hard-coded condition like `if (true)` won't pass.


=== step errors
title: Read the error, fix the code
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
Every developer makes typos, every day. The skill is not avoiding them: it's reading the error and fixing it quickly.

When something goes wrong, the Console shows a **red message** with:

- what went wrong, in JavaScript's words
- the **line number** where it happened
- in the Lab, a grey note that explains it in plain English

There are two kinds of error:

- JavaScript **can't read** the code at all, for example because a quote is missing. This is a **syntax error**, and *none* of the file runs.
- JavaScript can read it, but something fails **while running**, like a name it doesn't know. The lines above it still ran.

```js
console.log(shwoName);
// ReferenceError: shwoName is not defined
```

> The line number is where JavaScript *noticed* the problem. Sometimes the real mistake is on the line just above.
--- task
We added two broken lines at the bottom of your code. Look at the page: the grey notes are back, because one mistake stopped the whole file.

1. Read the red error and its line number. Fix that mistake.
2. A different error appears. Read it, and fix that one too.

When both are fixed, the badge above the title says **NBC**.
--- starter-append
// Step 10: two mistakes are hiding below. The Console will help you find them.
const network = "NBC;
document.querySelector("#network").textContent = netwrok;
--- hint
The first error is about a line JavaScript can't read. Count the quotes on that line: every string needs one quote at the start and one at the end.
--- hint
The first line needs a closing quote: `const network = "NBC";`. In the second line, check the spelling of the variable at the end: it should be `network`.
--- solution-append
// Step 10: two mistakes are hiding below. The Console will help you find them.
const network = "NBC";
document.querySelector("#network").textContent = network;
--- check
if (t.errors.length) {
  t.expect(!/netwrok/.test(t.errors[0]), "One fixed! Now the Console says `netwrok is not defined`: JavaScript doesn't know that name. Look at how the variable is spelled where it was created.");
  t.fail(`There's still an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Look at the line number it gives, and the line just above it.`);
}
const n = t.get("network");
t.expect(typeof n === "string", "I can't find the variable `network`. Keep the line `const network = \"NBC\";`, with both quotes.");
t.expect(n === "NBC", `\`network\` contains "${n}". It should be "NBC".`);
t.expect(t.text("#network") === "NBC", "The badge should say NBC. Keep the line that sets the text of `#network`.");
t.expect(t.text("#title"), "The title is empty. Keep your line from step 7 that fills `#title`.");
--- learned
You fixed two errors by reading the Console. Remember the difference: a syntax error stops the whole file, while other errors stop the code from that line on.
--- summary
Red errors name the problem and the line. A syntax error stops the whole file.
--- mistakes
- Fixing the line number in the message but not looking at the line above.
- Deleting the broken lines instead of fixing them.
--- teacher
Encourage students to read the error out loud. The grey explanation under each error is written for beginners.


=== step text-methods
title: Text that knows tricks
minutes: 10
slot: js
bonus: yes
--- explain
Strings come with built-in tools. Some are **properties**, facts about the string, written after a dot:

```js
const drink = "coffee";
console.log(drink.length);   // 6 (the number of characters)
```

Others are **methods**: actions the string can do. A method has brackets `( )` after its name:

```js
console.log(drink.toUpperCase());   // COFFEE
console.log(drink.toLowerCase());   // coffee
```

Methods don't change the variable itself. They give you a new value, and `drink` is still `"coffee"`.
--- task
1. Log the number of characters in `showName`.
2. At the very bottom, set `#title` to `showName` in capital letters.
--- hint
`.length` has no brackets, `.toUpperCase()` does. Both go straight after `showName`.
--- hint
```js
console.log(showName.length);
document.querySelector("#title").textContent = showName.toUpperCase();
```
--- solution-append
console.log(showName.length);
document.querySelector("#title").textContent = showName.toUpperCase();
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it and fix that line.`);
t.expect(t.logs.some((l) => l.text.trim() === "7"), "Log the length of the name: `console.log(showName.length);`. It should print 7.");
const title = t.text("#title");
t.expect(title !== "function toUpperCase() { [native code] }" && !/native code/.test(title), "Add the brackets: `showName.toUpperCase()`.");
t.expect(title === "FRIENDS", `The title says "${title}". Set it to \`showName.toUpperCase()\`, at the bottom of your code.`);
--- learned
Strings have properties like `.length` and methods like `.toUpperCase()`. You'll use `.toLowerCase()` on Day 6 to search for shows.
--- summary
`.length` counts characters; `.toUpperCase()` and `.toLowerCase()` change the case.


=== step random
title: A random episode
minutes: 15
slot: js
bonus: yes
--- explain
`Math.random()` gives a random number between 0 and 1, like `0.7281`. It's different every time the code runs.

To get a random whole number from 1 to 6, like a dice:

```js
const dice = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;
```

Step by step:

- `Math.random() * 6` gives a number between 0 and 5.999...
- `Math.floor( )` rounds **down**, so it becomes 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 or 5.
- `+ 1` makes it 1 to 6.
--- task
1. Create `randomEpisode`: a random whole number from 1 to `episodes`.
2. Put **Tonight's random pick: episode 137** (with your random number) in `#random`.

Press **Run again** above the preview a few times: the number changes.
--- hint
Use the dice example, with `episodes` instead of 6. Then a template literal for the text.
--- hint
```js
const randomEpisode = Math.floor(Math.random() * episodes) + 1;
document.querySelector("#random").textContent = `Tonight's random pick: episode ${randomEpisode}`;
```
--- solution-append
const randomEpisode = Math.floor(Math.random() * episodes) + 1;
document.querySelector("#random").textContent = `Tonight's random pick: episode ${randomEpisode}`;
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it and fix that line.`);
const seen = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  if (i > 0) await t.rerun();
  const txt = t.text("#random");
  t.expect(txt, "`#random` is empty. Put your sentence in it with `document.querySelector(\"#random\").textContent = ...`.");
  const m = txt.match(/\d+(\.\d+)?/g);
  t.expect(m, `\`#random\` says "${txt}". Put the number in it with \`\${randomEpisode}\`.`);
  const n = Number(m[m.length - 1]);
  t.expect(Number.isInteger(n), `I found ${n}: that's not a whole number. Round it down with \`Math.floor( )\`.`);
  t.expect(n >= 1 && n <= 236, `I found episode ${n}. The number should be from 1 to 236: multiply by \`episodes\` and add 1.`);
  seen.push(n);
}
t.expect(new Set(seen).size > 1, `I ran your page five times and always got ${seen[0]}. Use \`Math.random()\` so the number changes.`);
--- learned
`Math.random()` plus `Math.floor()` gives random whole numbers. Games, shuffles and "surprise me" buttons all start like this.
--- summary
`Math.floor(Math.random() * n) + 1` is a random whole number from 1 to n.


=== step else-if
title: Three choices with else if
minutes: 15
slot: js
bonus: yes
--- explain
With `else if` you can check more than two cases. JavaScript tries the conditions from top to bottom and runs the **first** block whose condition is true:

```js
if (cups >= 4) {
  console.log("Too much coffee!");
} else if (cups >= 2) {
  console.log("A normal day.");
} else {
  console.log("Time for a cup.");
}
```

With 5 cups, only the first message appears, even though 5 is also `>= 2`. Order matters: start with the strictest condition.
--- task
1. Create `const rating = 8.8;`.
2. Show a label in `#rating`: **★ Excellent** if rating is 9 or more, **★ Very good** if it's 8 or more, and **★ Worth a try** otherwise.

Try changing the rating to 9.5 and to 7 to see all three labels.
--- hint
Three blocks: `if (rating >= 9) { }`, then `else if (rating >= 8) { }`, then `else { }`. Each block sets the text of `#rating`.
--- hint
```js
const rating = 8.8;

if (rating >= 9) {
  document.querySelector("#rating").textContent = "★ Excellent";
} else if (rating >= 8) {
  document.querySelector("#rating").textContent = "★ Very good";
} else {
  document.querySelector("#rating").textContent = "★ Worth a try";
}
```
--- solution-append
const rating = 8.8;

if (rating >= 9) {
  document.querySelector("#rating").textContent = "★ Excellent";
} else if (rating >= 8) {
  document.querySelector("#rating").textContent = "★ Very good";
} else {
  document.querySelector("#rating").textContent = "★ Worth a try";
}
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Look for a missing bracket near the line it names.`);
t.expect(typeof t.get("rating") === "number", "Create the variable first: `const rating = 8.8;`.");
t.expect(/else\s+if\s*\(/.test(t.clean), "Use `else if (...)` for the middle case.");
const m = t.code.match(/\b(const|let)\s+rating\s*=\s*[^;\n]+/);
t.expect(m, "Create `rating` with `const rating = 8.8;`.");
const labels = {};
for (const r of [9.5, 8.8, 7]) {
  try {
    t.doc.querySelector("#rating").textContent = "";
    new t.win.Function(t.code.replace(m[0], m[1] + " rating = " + r))();
  } catch (e) { /* reported below */ }
  labels[r] = t.text("#rating");
}
await t.rerun();
t.expect(labels[8.8], "`#rating` is empty. Each block should set the text of `#rating`.");
t.expect(/very good/i.test(labels[8.8]), `With a rating of 8.8, \`#rating\` says "${labels[8.8]}". It should say Very good.`);
t.expect(/excellent/i.test(labels[9.5]), `With a rating of 9.5, \`#rating\` says "${labels[9.5]}". It should say Excellent: check the order, the \`>= 9\` test goes first.`);
t.expect(/worth a try/i.test(labels[7]), `With a rating of 7, \`#rating\` says "${labels[7]}". It should say Worth a try: that's the \`else\` block.`);
--- learned
`else if` adds as many cases as you need. JavaScript stops at the first true condition, so put the strictest one first. Tomorrow you'll wrap this in a function.
--- summary
`if / else if / else` chooses between several cases, top to bottom.
